Phase 2: Empirical Residual Covariance & Calibrated Uncertainty
Problem: Current simulation uses independent noise (90%) + shared form factor (10%) with fixed split ratios. Real residuals are correlated and athlete-specific.

2A. Compute Empirical Residual Covariance Matrix
File: New function in train.py or simulate.py
Changes:

After training, compute residuals on the training/validation set: residual_split = actual_split - pred_split for each of [swim, t1, bike, t2, run]
Compute per-distance covariance matrix Σ (5x5) from these residuals
Store Σ in ModelBundle.metadata["residual_covariance"]
Optionally decompose by distance category (sprint Σ vs standard Σ)
2B. Replace Independent Noise with Multivariate Normal Sampling
File: simulate.py
Changes:

In run_monte_carlo(), replace per-split independent noise generation with:

[e_swim, e_t1, e_bike, e_t2, e_run] ~ MVN(0, Σ_scaled)
Scale Σ per athlete using std_total_sec_24m / default_sigma ratio (heteroscedastic)
Scale by distance multiplier
Retire form_share parameter (MVN naturally captures the shared variance)
2C. Per-Split Sigma from Empirical Residuals
File: simulate.py → estimate_uncertainty()
Changes:

Replace hardcoded DEFAULT_SIGMA_SWIM=15, BIKE=45, RUN=30 with empirical per-split MAE from training residuals
Store per-distance empirical sigmas in bundle metadata
Keep per-athlete scaling via std_total_sec_24m (heteroscedastic)
Verification:

Coverage calibration test: for each split and total, check what fraction of actual values fall within the [p10, p90] simulation interval — target ~80%
Compare rank distributions before/after: should be smoother, less "spiked"
Phase 3: Resolve Pack Effect Double-Counting
Problem: Split models already use ema_bike_pack_7, ema_bike_gap_sec_7 etc. as features, so predicted bike time already partially accounts for the athlete's typical pack positioning. The simulation then adds pack effects on top.

3A. Quantify the Double-Counting
File: New analysis script
Changes:

Compute ablation: train split models WITH vs WITHOUT pack features
Measure how much split predictions change when pack features are removed
If significant: pack effects are baked in and simulation is double-counting
3B. Implement Clean Separation (Data-Driven Decision)
Train and backtest BOTH approaches, pick the winner:

Option A — "Ability-based" split models:

Train split models WITHOUT pack dynamics features (remove ema_swim_pack_7, ema_bike_pack_7, ema_bike_gap_sec_7 from split feature set)
These predict "intrinsic ability" — what the athlete would do solo
Let the simulation ADD all pack effects (no double-counting)
Option B — "Realized" split models:

Keep pack features in split models
Reduce sim pack adjustments to only model deviations from typical behavior
Implement both, backtest both on 2024 H2, pick whichever gives better Precision@10 + Spearman.

Verification:

Backtest both approaches on 2024 H2
Compare Spearman correlation and Precision@10
The correct approach should show more consistent results across different race types
